          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A rate measures how a property changes over time. Familiar examples include speed, which expresses distance traveled over time, and wage, which represents earnings per hour worked. In chemistry, the reaction rate measures how quickly reactants are consumed, or products are formed."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2794537740",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "We measure time-dependent changes in properties like volume, pressure, conductivity, or light absorption to determine reaction rates. For instance, in a reaction where reactant A converts to product B, the fading color of reactant A indicates its decreasing concentration, while the concentration of colorless product B increases."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2149591899",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The instantaneous rate is the reaction speed at any given moment, with the initial rate being the speed at the reaction's start. This is similar to a car's speed as it begins braking. Average reaction rates can be approximated through short-interval measurements or graphical methods, providing insights into the reaction's dynamics."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3358048111",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
